# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Local denylist read by scripts/lint-identifiers.sh -- never commit.
# It holds the real organisation/project/product strings that must not appear in
# this public repo, so committing it would publish exactly what it exists to
# catch. That is why the checker itself ships only STRUCTURAL rules.
.identifier-denylist

# Machine-local Claude Code settings -- never commit.
# This entry existed from the start and was silently INEFFECTIVE: the file was
# already tracked, and .gitignore does not apply to tracked files. It was
# untracked on 2026-08-04 (git rm --cached) after it was found publishing one
# machine's permission allowlist, including an AD username, to a public repo.
# Machine-level settings belong in ~/.claude/settings.json.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Agent worktrees -- never commit.
# The /implement pipeline creates one worktree per engineer agent under this
# path, each a full checkout of the repo, and merge-reviewer holds `git add -A`.
#
# Stated precisely, because the worst case is NOT the default: a live worktree
# registered by `git worktree add` still carries a valid `.git` pointer, and
# `git add -A` normally SKIPS such a directory with a warning rather than
# staging its contents. The case that actually bites is narrower -- a worktree
# left behind by a partially-failed cleanup, whose `.git` file is gone or
# corrupted, so what remains is an ordinary directory tree that `git add -A`
# will stage in full. This repo is public, so that is worth one line to prevent
# rather than reason about per run.
#
# Same class as the entry above: a path the harness writes into, which nothing
# in the pipeline is guaranteed to have removed at the moment something stages
# everything. Note that `git worktree list` reads `.git/worktrees/` metadata and
# is unaffected by this rule, so a registered stray worktree stays visible to
# the cleanup step in /implement 10a.
.claude/worktrees/
